27.2 Typography and hierarchy
Use one coherent modern sans-serif family (Inter, Geist or a similarly legible family). Use tabular numerals for values. Establish a visible scale; do not render every label and heading at the same size.
- Page title: typically 28–36px desktop, 24–30px mobile.
- Section title: 18–22px.
- Card heading: 15–18px, semibold.
- Body: 14–16px minimum for essential copy; prefer 15–16px.
- Chart labels/metadata: normally 12–13px, with zoom/help available; never use tiny labels for critical evidence.
- Hero metrics: responsive and large enough to read, but their labels and definitions must remain close.
- Helper text: concise and readable; no walls of tiny grey copy.
Use meaningful whitespace between page header, primary action, filters, content groups and footnotes. Use headings before cards. Avoid repeatedly nesting bordered cards inside bordered cards; use spacing, background tone and typographic hierarchy to group content.
27.3 Surface and card rules
- Rounded corners should be consistent and moderate, generally 8–14px, with larger radii reserved for hero panels or dialogs.
- No constant glow; no neon outline on every component; no floating unbounded pills; no excessive animated background.
- Use photographs only when they communicate crop/land context and are properly sized/cropped. Crop images need captions and accessible alt text where meaningful. Decorative imagery must not push key information below the fold.
- Use SVG/Lucide-style professional line icons, not emojis.
- Use a consistent icon size scale (16px inline, 18–20px controls, 22–24px section marks); do not use oversized icons to fill empty space.
- Data values, labels, units and provenance should form one readable cluster. Never detach a value's caveat two screens away.
- Use full-width tables when comparison is the point. Do not transform every table row into separate cards on desktop.
- A card should answer one clear question. Combine related weather facts in one coherent weather card rather than building a separate card for every number.
27.4 Grid, spacing and responsive layout
Use a responsive 12-column grid on wide desktop screens with consistent spacing. Main content should be close to full usable width, leaving balanced margins rather than an artificially narrow column. For wide tables and calendars, optimise for the viewport and only permit horizontal scrolling inside a clearly indicated data region when necessary—not on the whole page.

At each breakpoint verify: top navigation wrapping/collapse, table-to-list transformation, map tool placement, drawers converting to full-screen sheets on mobile, chart legibility, crop sequences reflowing vertically, sticky headers not covering content, and touch targets of at least 44×44 CSS pixels. Test desktop width 1440px, laptop 1280px, tablet 768px and mobile 390px/360px; these are representative test widths, not exclusive targets.
27.5 Shared components and their states
Build reusable components and specify the following states for every one:
- Button: default, hover, focus-visible, pressed, loading, disabled, success/error result.
- Input: empty, focused, filled, invalid, helper, read-only, saving, saved, save-failed.
- Select/combobox: closed, open, searching, no match, keyboard-selected, chosen, loading, failed.
- Card/list row: default, hover/clickable, selected, stale, loading, partial data, unavailable, actionable, resolved/archived.
- Tooltip/help popover: mouse hover and keyboard/focus/tap access; never require hover on mobile.
- Drawer/dialog: open, loading, content, validation error, save pending, save success, save failure, close with unsaved changes.
- Chart: loading, valid data, partial data, no data, failed source, selected point, accessible text/table alternative.
- Badge: icon + text + optional tooltip, with semantic label; no badge should be purely decorative.
- Empty state: explains why empty, what action creates content, and a real CTA.
- Toast: short outcome summary, optional Undo only when true undo is supported, auto-dismiss duration and accessible live-region announcement.
27.6 Motion rules
Use subtle 180–320ms interaction motion; wizard/page transitions may be around 250–450ms. Reversing a wizard step should move in the opposite direction. Use spring/layout motion only if it improves continuity. Motion must never delay a critical button or fake data progress. Respect prefers-reduced-motion, stop ambient motion in hidden tabs and never rely on animation alone to convey a state change.
28. Global undo/redo, command search and in-app guidance
28.1 Undo/redo behaviour
Provide global Undo and Redo at the far-left of the top bar before the logo, as requested in the historical brief. These buttons operate on a central action history of reversible domain changes such as editing a draft answer, renaming a farm, moving a field polygon vertex, changing a planned date or modifying an unsaved scenario. The history label should say e.g. “Undo: move boundary point” or “Redo: change irrigation assumption.”

Rules:
- Save an action snapshot or reversible command before mutation.
- Group rapid repeated changes (e.g. drag vertices) into one meaningful history action, not hundreds of entries.
- Do not include passive events like receiving new weather data as user-undoable actions.
- Warn before undoing an action whose reversal may create a stale analysis.
- If undo reverts an input used by an analysis, restore the input and mark affected results stale; do not mutate the historical report.
- Undoing a map geometry edit restores the prior unsaved geometry; undoing a saved geometry is a new versioned correction, not deletion of history.
- History must not allow cross-user actions or cross-farm actions to be replayed after the context changes.
- Disable controls and explain when the selected operation cannot be safely undone.
28.2 Global “?” guide
The attachment requests a persistent ? page guide. Include it consistently on major pages, wizard stages and farm details, but make it discreet. On activation, open a panel that explains: the current page's purpose; the visible sections and key numbers; what each action does; the recommended next 2–3 steps; and relevant glossary definitions. The guide content is contextual to the exact route and active mode, not a generic text dump. Allow closing, keyboard Escape, focus return and dismissible first-use tips. Do not let the guide block critical fields or cover save actions.
28.3 Global search / command launcher
A later but valuable enhancement: search farms, fields, crop plans, analysis reports, alerts, tasks and source/methodology entries by name. Include keyboard shortcut only if shown clearly and accessible. Results display type + parent context, so “Wheat” in Farm A cannot be confused with “Wheat” in Farm B. Selecting a result must route to the exact entity context. Search results must obey user permissions and should not index private report content into third-party services without consent.
29. Map workspace: complete interaction and geometry contract
The map and drawing workflow is a core product capability, not an optional decoration. Provide it both during Add Farm/field setup and later within My Farms, with shared geometry logic so boundaries cannot diverge between workflows.
29.1 Map page layout
Desktop: map canvas fills the majority of the page (roughly 65–75% width); a contextual right-side inspector (roughly 25–35%) shows selected farm/field details, drawing instructions, layer controls and save status. A compact left list or top selector provides farm/field navigation. The exact proportions should adapt to viewport and inspector visibility. Never shrink the map into a small card surrounded by unused space.

Top map toolbar: search address/place, selected farm/field breadcrumb, map layers, basemap choice where available, fit-to-selection, locate-me, measurement/area units and Help. Drawing toolbar: Select, Draw field boundary, Edit vertices, Draw exclusion area/hole, Import boundary, Undo, Redo, Delete selected (with confirmation), Cancel edits and Save changes. Disable controls that do not make sense in the current mode and show their tooltip/reason.

Right inspector sections: field/farm identity; area/perimeter; location/centroid; boundary quality and unsaved changes; selected map layer and coverage note; field-specific crop/soil/water metadata; action links to “Open field overview,” “Plan crop rotations,” “Run Deep Analysis” and “View evidence.” The inspector must show source/measurement limitations for any overlay and never imply that a basemap is an agronomic sensor.
29.2 Initial map loading and world bounds
- Render a single world; do not repeat the world horizontally. For Leaflet use a no-wrap tile configuration, world bounds, no world-copy jumping and a suitable minimum zoom. For MapLibre, disable world copies and set valid geographic bounds. Respect the chosen library's current official docs.
- Initial view fits the whole world without unnecessary clipping, then smoothly zooms to a supplied saved farm/field if one exists. If a saved boundary exists, fit the geometry with comfortable padding and preserve a sensible zoom cap.
- On resize, drawer open/close, or entering a hidden tab, call the map library's resize/invalidate-size operation; prevent blank or grey maps.
- Validate longitude in [-180, 180] and latitude in [-90, 90]; clamp search/fly-to requests to valid coordinates and explain invalid manual values.
- Provide zoom buttons and trackpad/mouse-wheel navigation; support pinch zoom on touch. Keyboard users need accessible map controls and a non-map list/form alternative.
- Map attribution must remain visible. Do not promise a public tile provider has 100% uptime; respect its usage policy and provide a graceful failure state.
29.3 Ways to add a farm or field
Support these routes where data/service capability allows:
1. Search by place/address: user enters a place, selects a result, map animates to it and shows the chosen place. Ask them to confirm before saving.
2. Use my location: request browser permission only after user action. On denial or unsupported devices, keep manual search and coordinate entry available. Do not persist precise GPS position or upload it without clear user action/consent.
3. Enter coordinates: latitude/longitude input with clear decimal-degree labels, validation and a preview pin.
4. Draw boundary: user clicks/taps points around the actual field, closes the polygon, reviews area and names the field.
5. Import geometry: support a defined and tested set of formats (initially GeoJSON/KML; other formats such as zipped shapefiles only after a reliable parser and validation are included). Show filenames, detected polygon count, coordinate reference assumptions, invalid features and field names before import. Never silently drop failed features.
6. Select a detected/predefined field boundary: only if a real provider/dataset supports it in the chosen geography. The interface must disclose how the boundaries were obtained and allow correction. Do not show selectable field outlines as though they exist if no such integration is active.
For every path, the farm name must remain the name the farmer entered. Searching an address, dropping a pin or selecting a boundary must never overwrite the farm/field name automatically.
29.4 Draw polygon interaction — precise sequence
1. User selects Draw field boundary. Enter explicit draw mode and show a short instruction (“Click around the outside edge of the field. Select the first point to close the boundary.”).
2. The first click creates vertex 1. Subsequent clicks add vertices and live connecting edges. A faint fill shows the current polygon. Show live approximate area only once enough valid vertices exist, marked “draft estimate” until closed.
3. A line preview follows the pointer/touch to the next potential vertex. Keep it subtle; never obscure satellite imagery/basemap labels.
4. Clicking the first vertex closes the polygon. A “Finish drawing” button provides a more accessible alternative. Escape cancels the drawing with a confirmation if vertices would be lost; Backspace removes only the last vertex (except when focus is in a text field); Undo removes the last geometry operation.
5. After closing, show area and perimeter in the selected unit, vertex count, field name input, and validation summary. Allow user to refine the shape before saving.
6. Dragging an existing vertex changes a draft geometry; display updated area in real time with a debounce. Keep a clear selected vertex marker and maintain pointer capture so the point does not jump.
7. Adding/removing vertices maintains valid polygon ring order. If the shape self-intersects, display the offending edges, explain why it may produce incorrect area and disable Save until fixed or corrected. Do not silently reorder vertices or change the shape.
8. If two field polygons overlap beyond the agreed tolerance, show both outlines and the estimated overlap. Offer “Review overlap,” not an automatic change. Shared borders can touch and small coordinate artefacts may be treated with a documented tolerance.
9. Exclusion areas / holes: allow the farmer to cut out buildings, ponds, roads or non-cultivated patches from an otherwise valid field only after explicit selection of “Add exclusion area.” Distinguish the field exterior from internal holes visually and in GeoJSON geometry. Recalculate usable area and label gross versus excluded/net area.
10. Save opens a confirm/review state summarising field name, area, parent farm, boundary edits and implications. Save must be atomic: either the valid geometry and metadata are committed together or neither is. Cancel returns to the previous persisted geometry. If there are unsaved changes and the user navigates away, ask whether to save, discard or stay.
11. After a successful saved boundary edit, create a new geometry version, recalculate derived area/centroid, invalidate affected spatial caches, and mark field-level analyses that depend on spatial coverage as stale. Keep past reports linked to the old boundary version.
29.5 Geometry, area and quality rules
- Use GeoJSON-compatible WGS84 coordinate storage, with a suitable geodesic/projected calculation for area and distance. Do not calculate hectares from raw latitude-longitude degree spans.
- Persist original imported geometry and normalized geometry separately where transformations are made. Store coordinate reference system when known.
- Store geometry revision, author/source, created timestamp, and edit reason where helpful.
- Validate empty polygons, fewer than three distinct vertices, duplicate adjacent vertices, invalid ring closure, self-intersection, out-of-range coordinates, unsupported geometry types, non-finite coordinates and implausible area.
- A farm may contain multiple fields. Define whether fields may overlap by design (e.g. nested management zones) or whether overlapping production fields are normally warning states. Never sum overlapping field areas into a portfolio total without a disclosed deduplication strategy.
- If a field is too small relative to a dataset pixel/grid, show the resolution mismatch and lower confidence in spatial representativeness. Do not upsample a single coarse observation into apparent field-level detail.
- If satellite images/cloud-free scenes are unavailable, keep the map usable with the best available basemap and explain the limitation. No fake NDVI/satellite raster.
29.6 Map layer switcher
Only list layers that are implemented and have real data available for the selected period/place. Potential layers include base map; field outlines; actual optical image; actual NDVI/EVI; rainfall; elevation/slope; soil properties; and observation-quality mask. Every layer entry has: source/provider, product name, units/legend, observation date/time window, nominal spatial resolution, processing method and quality caveat.

When the user switches layers: keep the same selected field and map extent; show layer-specific legend and loading state; handle no-data/cloud-contaminated scenes; do not change the farm boundary itself; and update the evidence metadata. If two layers cannot be meaningfully combined, disable the combination with a short reason instead of adding unreadable blended colors.
29.7 Map selection and map-to-page navigation
Clicking a field outline selects it and opens/updates the right inspector. A selected outline uses a clear solid accent border and restrained transparent fill; unselected fields use quieter borders and accessible labels. Clicking a field from a list flies to and selects the same geometry. “View detail” opens the exact field page. “Crop rotation” opens that field's active rotation or starts a plan if none exists. “Deep Analysis” opens the workspace preselected to that field. “Fit all fields” frames all valid field boundaries. When map edits are unsaved, changing field prompts to save/discard/stay. Preserve map selection on return from detail pages when sensible.
29.8 Map error and empty states
- No farm yet: show a clear empty state and “Add a farm.”
- Farm has no boundary: show the pin/coordinates and offer “Draw boundary” and “Import boundary.”
- Tile provider down: maintain editing if geometry tools can function on cached/background blank coordinates; show “Map tiles could not load” and manual coordinates/list fallback.
- Geocoder down: preserve typed query and expose manual coordinate or pan-and-select alternative.
- GPS denied: no repeated permission prompts; explain how to choose a location manually.
- Invalid geometry: highlight the exact error and the action to fix it.
- Import failure: show file-specific errors and allow retry without discarding unsaved form data.
- Partial geodata: clearly separate unavailable layers from a map rendering failure.
30. Page-by-page redesign contract
For every screen below, the builder must implement the named regions in the specified order. When a screen has more content than fits, use a clear hierarchy, meaningful grouping and progressive disclosure, not tiny text. Each primary button must have a real action, pending state and success/failure feedback.
30.1 First visit, workspace creation and empty Home
Purpose: establish where the farm is and let the user get started without a giant form or a promotional landing page pretending to be the working dashboard.

Layout: a clean headline (“Understand your land. Plan better rotations.”), concise explanation, one primary action “Add your first farm”, secondary “Explore a clearly labelled demo farm” if a deterministic demo is implemented, and an optional “Continue setup” if a draft exists. Use a realistic agricultural image as a restrained crop/landscape backdrop only if it does not reduce contrast or consume the viewport. No fake farm metrics.

Interaction: “Add your first farm” opens the Add Farm wizard at the first step. “Demo farm” loads labelled illustrative data and persists an explicit demo state; demo values can never be confused with the user's real farm. “Continue setup” resumes at the correct first incomplete required step or the review page if required answers are complete. If the user has a saved farm but no current report, Home prioritises “Run your first assessment” / “Plan crop rotations” rather than onboarding cards.
30.2 Add Farm and Add Field wizard
Create distinct entities: a farm is the overall operation and a field is a spatial management unit inside a farm. Do not use “farm” and “field” interchangeably in labels or calculations.

Farm wizard steps: (1) name and optional note; (2) country/region/location search, map pin or coordinate entry; (3) draw/import farm boundary or save location and add boundary later; (4) add fields now or later; (5) review location, area, selected units and data/service limitations; (6) save.

Field wizard steps: (1) field name; (2) boundary draw/import/select supported field outline; (3) optional crop in ground and planting date; (4) soil/water knowledge—known, estimated or not known; (5) crop history; (6) review and save.

All steps: autosave locally as drafts; show stage counter; persistent Back; Save & exit; optional Skip only where allowed; validate inputs inline; keep name values independent of map search; no surprise overwrite of existing fields; review all collected information before creation. If creation partially fails, the UI reports what was saved and resumes safely without duplicate farms/fields on retry.
30.3 Home — daily farm tracker
Page hierarchy:
1. Page title and compact selected-farm selector, with “Add farm” / “View all farms” secondary actions.
2. A main status panel showing farm name, location, current condition label (Good / Needs attention / Alert / Insufficient data), last evidence update time, active crop and plan phase if known.
3. Current conditions/weather card with current/recent values or forecast if a real forecast source is connected; always label observation, forecast and reanalysis distinctly. Show a short range or context such as “rainfall below typical for this period” only when the baseline is computed.
4. Crop rotation at a glance: crop photo, current crop, sequence, season/year, next transition window, days until transition only if dates are supported, and a clickable sequence leading to the correct field/rotation.
5. Water Intelligence summary: plain-language water balance/status, supported recent precipitation, modelled crop demand and data freshness; advanced detail opens Water Intelligence.
6. Soil Intelligence summary: a supported soil property or condition indicator and whether the value is a lab measurement, mapped estimate or calculation. Advanced detail opens Soil & Land.
7. “What changed since your last check?” short ranked events, only if comparison evidence exists.
8. Recommended next action(s), with reason, due window and action state. If nothing is justified, say “No immediate action identified from the available data.”
9. Active alerts only. Do not reserve an empty alert block; no critical status from a silent fallback.
10. Optional portfolio watch when multiple farms/fields exist: list each relevant field and its specific condition.
Click reactions: farm selector updates all sections together; crop sequence opens the matching rotation stage; weather values open their evidence; water/soil summaries open detail tabs; alert opens evidence and affected field; action opens task detail; report history opens dated report. “Start New Analysis” should not be the same action as Deep Analysis or rotation planning; use labels “Plan Crop Rotations” and “Run Deep Analysis” as context-appropriate actions.

Simple mode: short status sentences, 1–3 actions and the key crop/rotation view. Advanced mode: source timestamps, telemetry, supported 30-day trends, water/soil detail, sensor resolution, scientific explanations and trend thresholds. Keep Advanced content expandable rather than dumping every variable onto Home.
30.4 My Farms portfolio page
Page hierarchy: page title + subtitle, search, status filters, sort, list/table toggle if useful, “Add farm” primary action, then farm table/list. Do not surround the table with multiple nested cards.

Desktop columns: farm thumbnail/mark, farm name and location, total boundary area and number of fields, current crop-plan state, farm status, last analysis with freshness, active alert count, and a row action menu. Values are sourced from records; avoid hardcoded sample farms in a real account. On mobile, each farm becomes a compact stacked row with status and key actions; it must not overflow horizontally.

Actions: open farm; rename; edit farm information; manage fields/map; duplicate configuration only with a clear explanation that data/history are not duplicated unless selected; archive; delete with consequence summary and confirmation; export. Search must search names and location labels. Sorting/filtering must operate on actual records.

Click behaviour: clicking a farm row opens that farm's Overview, not a generic page. The active/selected farm is reflected in global state. Deletion checks linked fields/plans/reports; explain what will be removed and whether records in cloud/backups are also deleted. If deletion is irreversible, do not claim it is undoable.

Advanced-only portfolio information can include total managed area, active farms, fields by status, rotation reviews due and alerts. Do not compute an “average health” figure from incompatible or missing component metrics without a documented aggregation model.
30.5 Farm detail workspace
Use a consistent farm heading region with: breadcrumb My Farms > [Farm]; farm name; location and area; status with text; last analysis; actions menu; field/farm selector when needed. A map thumbnail/mini-map can sit beside the heading, but it must be clickable to open the full boundary workspace.

Desktop sub-navigation can be a compact left rail inside the farm workspace. On tablet/mobile convert it to a labelled horizontal scroll menu or dropdown. Do not copy an oversized sidebar onto all global pages.

Required sections:
- Overview / Farm Tracker: summary from Home with expanded details.
- Fields & Map: fields list, boundary editor, create/import field, selected field status and comparison.
- Crop Rotation Plan: active rotation for selected field; if multiple fields, make the field selector explicit and do not combine them into one sequence.
- Soil & Land: properties table, texture composition chart, pH/reference ranges only where relevant, slope/erosion context, depth and source; lab measurements take precedence only when appropriate and current.
- Water Intelligence: crop water demand, rainfall/irrigation contributions, net water balance, model assumptions, data history and calculation explorer.
- Weather & Climate: actual current forecast from an identified source, historical weather/reanalysis with timestamps, climate baseline and risks by crop window.
- Vegetation & Field Change: satellite imagery or vegetation index only when genuinely retrieved and quality-screened; display scene date, sensor, resolution and cloud/no-data states.
- Actions & Observations: planned/done/overdue actions, farmer log and record details.
- Past Analyses: report list, dated scores and comparison.
- Farm Settings: editable farm details, unit preferences where global, default practices, data permissions and deletion/archive.
Each section starts with a human-readable summary then offers advanced expansion. Standard rows and charts link to evidence. Do not duplicate different copies of the same metric with conflicting formulas.
30.6 Crop Rotation Planner — question wizard
The planner is the primary tool for “Which rotations fit this field?” It is not Deep Analysis and it is not a What-If scenario.

Wizard contract:
- One decision/question per stage; use conditional follow-up only when it changes the model.
- Header: breadcrumb/context, progress text (“Step 3 of 7 required”), progress bar and Save & exit.
- Main column: clear question (32–48px desktop, responsive), helper line explaining why the question matters, exactly one principal input group and one primary action.
- Side rail on desktop may list broad categories, not future unanswered questions with confusing status.
- Back is always available; keyboard Back is supported without stealing browser shortcuts from text inputs. Next/Continue is disabled only when an answer is genuinely required and invalid.
- Single-choice cards can auto-advance only after a safe short delay and cancellation route; prefer explicit Continue for consequential or ambiguous inputs.
- All drafts save locally on every answer and sync only according to consent/identity architecture. The UI says Saved, Saving or Save failed honestly.
- Optional unknowns use “I don't know” and “Skip this question”; these are explicit states, not an empty string.
- Every input has an accessible label and correct units.
Required question groups: field/location selection; soil texture/known soil info; water source and irrigation type/reliability; crop history; planning horizon and cropping intensity; priorities. Optional accuracy group: pH/lab data, drainage/slope/tillage, pest/disease history, fertilizer/residue practices, crop exclusions/inclusions, water salinity, yields, available planting windows and any other factor the actual model supports.

The review screen shows all inputs grouped under Farm/Field, Soil, Water, Crop History, Management, Planning and Priorities. Each row shows value, units, provenance, last-confirmed date and inline Edit. Editing an upstream value identifies which later answers become irrelevant, which analysis cache is invalidated and why.
30.7 Crop Rotation Planner — result and detail pages
Result page order:
1. field/farm context and analysis timestamp;
2. best-ranked valid rotation summary;
3. one wide three-part rotation card: score and headline trade-offs on the left, crop sequence in the centre, why-it-fits summary on the right;
4. priority/component score summary and evidence confidence (not conflated);
5. Current field context: current crop, plan stage, next transition, supported water/soil signals;
6. Benefits & Risks, including risk level, affected crop/stage, evidence or mechanism, mitigation and what is uncertain;
7. Ranked alternatives and a working compare selector;
8. schedule/timeline and review trigger;
9. additional supporting insights; deep details remain collapsible.
Rotation sequence cards: use realistic crop photography, crop name below image, year/season labels above, arrows or a visible directional link between stages, duration and planting/harvest windows where supported. Hover/tap displays duration in the field, botanical family and relevant stage note. On mobile, use a vertical or horizontally scrollable sequence region with clear control; never make the whole page overflow.

Scoring and plan labels: describe the overall figure as a match/suitability score out of 100, not predicted yield or likelihood of success. Display component scores (suitability, water, soil health, resilience, productivity, diversity) only when each is defined, computable and tested. Every metric has a definition, basis, formula/version in advanced detail, source/provenance, period and confidence where applicable.

Ranked candidates: show top 3 expanded initially; remaining candidates are compact and expandable or paginated. Advanced mode can expose the full valid ranked set with filters. Add a “Not recommended under current assumptions” group only for actual candidates tested and rejected, including the hard constraint or factor causing rejection. Do not call a plan “valid” if it violates crop-family return intervals, growing windows or required water constraints.

Head-to-head comparison: farmer selects two rotations (three only if layout remains readable). Display score, duration, crop sequence, each priority component, water demand/availability, soil trajectory, nitrogen assumptions, crop-family break, disease-cycle considerations, seasonal climate fit, workload/timing and evidence/confidence. At the bottom give an explicit conditional conclusion: “Plan A is the stronger match for your current water-saving priority; Plan B scores better on productivity potential under the stated assumptions.” Show when the difference is too small or unsupported to justify a confident winner.

Detailed rotation screen: selected plan title and active/saved status; crop sequence; year vs season distinction; duration explanation; planting and harvest windows; crop-specific climate conditions; risks/mitigations; water demand vs effective rainfall and reported irrigation; soil and nitrogen/organic matter trajectory where the model is validated; review-by date and trigger; source and assumptions drawer; print/export one-page field plan. Every button must work.
30.8 Farm Intelligence / Deep Analysis workspace
Purpose: “How are the selected farm and its fields doing, what changed, what risks need attention, and which improvements deserve consideration?” It is a monitoring/diagnostic workflow, not a replacement for rotation planning.

Header: Farm Intelligence; selected farm; field scope (All fields or selected subset); latest report timestamp; current assessment freshness; primary Run Deep Analysis action; schedule status/menu; reports/history link. Show a compact evidence coverage strip and a warning if critical sources are missing or stale.

Before running, open a short preflight panel: selected scope; relevant profile/input changes; data sources expected; last confirmed irrigation/management routine; whether the user wants to update it now. Offer “Use last confirmed routine” with its timestamp and “Update practices.” Do not silently assume the stored routine is current.

Run state sequence:
1. Validate selected farm/field IDs, boundaries, profile completeness and permission to run.
2. Freeze a run-input snapshot (farm/field profile versions, crop plan versions, practice records, data time windows, sources, model/crop KB versions).
3. Queue/execute required data refresh jobs with per-source timeout, cache and retry policy.
4. Validate each response and unit; handle data gaps/fill values; check spatial and temporal adequacy; store provenance.
5. Calculate supported field-level indicators and field assessments.
6. Calculate a farm-wide summary using the declared aggregation methodology; show field scores separately and never allow aggregation to obscure severe field-specific alerts.
7. Generate evidence-linked findings and actions through structured templates/rules. AI may paraphrase and parse user language, but cannot fabricate measurements or invent causes.
8. Validate each generated explanatory claim against structured calculations and sources. If validation fails, omit the claim or mark it unverified.
9. Persist a dated immutable report and update current-report pointer only after successful finalisation.
10. Display report status, coverage, partial failures and “what changed since the previous report.”
30.9 Deep Analysis report — full hierarchy
1. Report header: farm name; date and timezone; scope and fields included; run status (Complete / Partial / Failed); model/version; data window; evidence freshness.
2. Farm condition summary: farm-wide score/index only if validated; otherwise a summary of supported component indicators. Explanation, confidence and aggregation method.
3. Field-by-field results: each field's condition, separate score/indicators, top finding, alert severity, evidence quality and link to field detail.
4. Priority findings: sorted by severity, confidence, urgency and actionability, not just by visual drama. Each includes “What we noticed”, “Why it matters”, evidence, date/range, uncertainty and “What to do next.”
5. Water intelligence: precipitation period and source; forecast/reanalysis/observation distinction; estimated crop demand and irrigation records if supported; water-balance method; missing practice data; limitations.
6. Soil and land: available properties, depth, source, measured vs mapped distinction, drainage/slope context, missing lab measurements and confidence.
7. Weather/climate exposure: heat/dry spell/frost/flooding exposure only with crop-specific thresholds and relevant time windows; forecasts separate from long-term baseline.
8. Vegetation and field change: only real retrieved imagery/index values; scene dates; cloud quality; spatial-resolution explanation; do not infer exact disease/nutrient diagnosis from greenness alone.
9. Management-practice context: user-confirmed irrigation, planting, fertiliser, residue and scouting history; mark unknown or stale routines visibly.
10. Potential risks: risk level, causal mechanism or plausible pathways, evidence supporting it, confidence and mitigation. Distinguish “possible risk” from confirmed event.
11. Recommended actions: priority, reason, field, due window, dependencies, estimated effort only if supported, action buttons and evidence link.
12. Data gaps and improvement opportunities: missing inputs and how to provide them; predicted effect on reliability expressed qualitatively unless validated quantitatively.
13. Change comparison: compare with previous comparable report; split difference drivers into changed farmer inputs, new observation/data, changed geometry/crop plan, different model version or missing-data change.
14. Methods and provenance: expandable full source registry, variable values and units, timestamps, calculation steps, thresholds, assumptions, model version and quality flags.
15. Run summary and audit: stage outcomes, source failures, partial computations, run ID and report creation timestamp. Never expose secret tokens or unnecessary sensitive logs.
The report must distinguish farm condition, evidence confidence, data readiness and recommendation priority as different concepts. A single composite farm health score is not mandatory if it cannot be scientifically justified. The requirement is that both farm-wide and field-level views exist; the farm-wide view can summarise component metrics until a defensible validated composite is available.
30.10 Report history and comparison
Report history is a dated list or timeline per farm, with field filters and clear Complete/Partial/Failed states. Every report is immutable after creation except for an added annotation/correction record. A new run creates a new report; it does not rewrite an old one.

Selecting two reports opens a comparison with: farm and field scope differences; scores only where comparable; changed inputs; data source/product/timestamp differences; model/crop database version differences; metric-by-metric changes; new/resolved alerts; recommended actions added/completed/deferred; and missing values introduced by failed sources. If a method changes, show “not directly comparable” or recompute both under a common method when valid source data is available.

Allow export to PDF/CSV after confirming its scope; include a report ID, generated date, source caveats and model version. Do not include more precise location detail than appropriate for the selected report-sharing/privacy setting.
30.11 Automation and schedule settings
Automation setup uses a clearly separated flow:
1. Choose farm and fields.
2. Choose frequency: daily, every 3 days, weekly or a custom supported schedule. Explain that the schedule is a re-evaluation frequency, not a promise that every source publishes new observations at that cadence.
3. Choose preferred run window and timezone derived from farm/user settings. Handle daylight-saving transitions correctly where relevant.
4. Review which inputs will be sent to the backend for the scheduled job, why they are required, how long they are retained, where deletion applies and what stays local.
5. Confirm the last known irrigation/management routine. Ask whether it remains valid. If unchanged, record confirmation timestamp. If changed, capture new inputs and preserve the previous value as historical.
6. Configure material-change notifications, ordinary report-ready notices and repeated-alert suppression.
7. Review and explicitly enable schedule.
Scheduled run logic: a backend scheduler enqueues jobs only for enabled and consented schedules. It loads the minimum permitted snapshot; refreshes eligible sources; skips data-dependent recomputation if relevant inputs/data have not changed and policy permits; otherwise runs the analysis; stores source statuses and report; and sends notification only according to user settings. If cloud input is missing or consent expired, do not improvise with local data the backend cannot read—mark the job as waiting for updated device sync or suspended and explain it.

Rainfall rule: only use newly available rainfall observations/reanalysis from an appropriate source and mark their type/date. Forecast rain may influence forward-looking planning but is not recorded as observed rainfall. Rainfall does not prove irrigation did not happen. Unless the farmer records an irrigation event or a supported sensor establishes it, irrigation remains unknown/reported routine; it is not automatically subtracted from the record based only on rainfall.

Schedule lifecycle: Draft → Awaiting consent → Enabled → Queued → Running → Complete/Partial/Failed → Retrying (if eligible) → Paused/Suspended/Disabled. Preserve prior runs. Include Run now, Pause, Resume, Change schedule, View last report and Disable actions. Confirm disabling and explain that existing reports remain. Run now must respect per-user/field rate limits and show whether it queues or executes immediately.

Idempotency and duplicates: one logical schedule occurrence generates at most one final report per scope and input snapshot unless user explicitly reruns. Retries reuse a job ID/idempotency key. Concurrent runs for the same farm/field/window are de-duplicated or safely serialized.
30.12 Notification centre
Group notifications into actionable alerts, report completion/failure, schedule health, upcoming task and system/data notices. Each notification has a stable ID, type, created time, status, related farm/field, deep link and short plain-language description. Acknowledge/read/dismiss/snooze are distinct actions; dismissing an alert does not resolve the underlying condition. Define retention and prevent repeated copies of the same alert unless severity or evidence materially changes.

When clicked, route to the exact report, evidence, task or field and preserve its origin. Notification detail in Advanced Mode may show observed/reference value, anomaly, dataset and timestamp. Simple Mode starts with human-readable wording and a direct next step. If an alert has insufficient confidence, use “Check this” / “Possible issue” rather than definitive wording.
30.13 Scenario Simulator
Header explains “Test a possible change without editing the real farm.” User selects a farm/field and a baseline analysis/rotation. Left/control area includes scenario name, controls for supported variables (e.g. temperature/rainfall shifts or water availability only where modelling supports it), presets, reset-to-baseline and saved scenarios. Right/results area shows baseline vs scenario, rank changes, component delta, risk drivers and a textual explanation.

The simulator must state the scenario time range and assumptions. It must not relabel scenario numbers as forecasts. Unsupported control/variable combinations should be disabled with a reason. Results must be deterministic for identical inputs and model versions. Save/duplicate/rename/delete scenario actions all work. “Apply to real plan” opens a review diff of changed assumptions and requires explicit confirmation, then creates a new version; it never mutates historical scenarios or reports.
30.14 Calendar & Actions
The page combines time-based crop stages and actionable farm work while retaining their different data types. Header: title, farm scope, field filter, current month/week/agenda toggle, Today, date navigation, create task and filters. Calendar grid must use screen width efficiently and not be trapped in a small box with unused margins.

Month grid has fixed-position previous/next controls; centered month/year; accessible day headers; clear crop labels within relevant cells; crop-color palette with readable contrast; visually different but legible marks when multiple farms have events on the same day; selected day state; and a textual agenda panel. Do not rely on background color alone to distinguish overlapping farm events.

Selected-date panel lists events chronologically: crop stage/planting/harvest window, recommended task, scheduled Deep Analysis, farmer record and any alert. Each row displays the event type, farm/field, date basis (planned/observed/modelled), status and actions. Clicking an event opens detail; clicking a crop uses the canonical deep link to the exact rotation stage; completing a task updates the action record and report context, not its underlying evidence. Dates derived from a model are windows, not exact promises.

Support create/edit/reschedule/complete/defer/cancel for tasks with audit history. Conflicts with the crop plan should trigger a warning. Changing planting/harvest dates marks affected rotation timelines and analyses stale. Today returns to the actual local date for the farm or selected timezone and does not reset unrelated filters.
30.15 Data & Trust Centre
Page purpose: “Show me what Field Shift knows, where it came from, and how it reached a conclusion.”

Top overview: active/inactive/degraded connectors and last successful retrieval for the selected farm/time window. Dataset table fields: product/source, variable, actual connection status (Integrated / Planned / Unavailable / Degraded), native resolution, time coverage/cadence, last retrieved/observation date, quality notes, license/attribution and official docs link.

A value-level Evidence Drawer contains actual raw/normalised value, units and standardisation; source/type; source timestamp and retrieval time; spatial footprint vs field size; quality flags; fallback chain; transformation and formula; model version; confidence basis and limitations. A calculation explorer shows formula in readable form, each input substituted with actual values/units, outputs/intermediate values, assumptions and references. It does not claim a named formula unless the implementation is actually that formula.

If a source connector is only planned, label it Planned and do not populate live status. Do not claim NASA, SMAP or Sentinel-2 data were fetched merely because a banner says NASA. The registry itself must be generated from the connectors and provenance records actually used in each run, not from a static list that overstates the product.
30.16 Settings
Settings is divided into sections, not a long flat form:
- Display: Simple/Advanced mode toggle; light/dark mode only if supported; text/units and timezone; motion preference.
- Farm defaults: default farm, default units, inherited practice settings with scope clearly stated.
- Automation: enabled schedules, cadence, timezone, last run status, consent/data snapshot, change/pause/disable.
- Notifications: alert types, report-ready, failures, daily/weekly summary and quiet hours.
- Data and privacy: what stays local, what syncs, scheduled snapshot details, export, clear local data, revoke cloud schedule consent, cloud report deletion and retention explanation.
- Connections: current source status and any account/permission needs; no fake connection status.
- About and methodology: engine version, crop knowledge base version, sources and limitations.
Remove billing from Settings unless a real implemented billing system is explicitly required later. All settings copy should use clear plain language. Every save has visible state; changes that affect an active schedule are explained before commit.
31. Canonical data model and event contracts
The prior data model must be expanded sufficiently for implementation. Use typed schemas and stable IDs. Exact database technology may vary; semantic contracts may not.
31.1 Core entities
- User/LocalWorkspace: workspace ID, owner/auth subject if applicable, created date, locale/timezone, preferences, data mode and consent records. Anonymous local-only use must remain possible if chosen; scheduled remote processing requires a secure identity/signed-device binding.
- Farm: ID, display name, region/country, timezone, default units, optional farm-level polygon, centroid, notes, created/updated timestamps, archived/deleted state and version.
- Field: ID, farm ID, display name, geometry version, area and method, centroid, soil profile references, current crop/rotation ID, profile completeness and timestamps.
- GeometryVersion: ID, farm/field ID, geometry, CRS, area/perimeter, source/import method, validation results, effective timestamp, created timestamp and superseded version ID.
- FarmPractice / FieldPractice: practice type, structured value/unit, schedule/frequency, effective date range, scope, provenance, original user text if supplied, parsed output, ambiguity flags, user confirmation timestamp and stale/unknown status.
- CropHistoryEvent: field ID, crop ID/family, start/end or season, dates certainty (exact, estimated, unknown), source, disease/pest notes and user-confirmed yields if provided.
- CropKnowledgeVersion: crop ID, family, duration bounds, crop coefficients/thresholds/source references, planting/harvest conditions, water/nutrient traits, host-family constraints, evidence quality and version. No agronomic constant without a source/reasoning record.
- AnalysisInputSnapshot: stable snapshot ID and hash; farm/field/geometry/profile/practice/history version IDs; source payload references and time windows; user priorities; engine/crop database versions; consent state; created timestamp.
- DataObservation: source/product/variable, raw and normalised value, unit, start/end timestamp, retrieval timestamp, geometry/pixel/grid information, QC flags, source type (observation/model/reanalysis/estimate), cache ID and provenance ID.
- DerivedMetric: metric key, value/unit, time window, calculation ID/version, input references, uncertainty/confidence basis, availability state and explanation keys.
- RotationPlan: ID, farm/field, created date, plan status, crop sequence/stages, active version, priority weights, source snapshot, total duration, review triggers and user decision state.
- RotationCandidate: analysis ID, candidate ID, sequence, season schedule, hard-constraint checks, total match and component scores, risks/trade-offs, confidence and stable rank explanation.
- AnalysisRun: run ID/type/scope, trigger (manual, scheduled, recompute, scenario), status, input snapshot ID, source outcomes, stage events, retry count, start/end timestamps, engine versions, result/report ID and failure classification.
- DeepAnalysisReport: immutable report ID/version, run ID, farm summary, per-field results, findings, actions, data gaps, previous-report comparison references and export metadata.
- Alert: ID, affected entity, type/severity/status, condition/threshold version, evidence references, first/last seen, suppression group, acknowledgment/resolution history and destination route.
- Action/Task: ID, affected farm/field/plan/finding, title and description, rationale, source references, status, due window, created-by, completion evidence/notes, deferral reason and revision history.
- ObservationJournalEntry: ID, farm/field, event type, narrative, normalized values/units, original user text if applicable, attachments, date/location confidence, author and privacy state.
- Scenario: ID, baseline run/plan version, modified input deltas, units, assumptions, name, creation date, result, status and scenarioOnly=true marker.
- Schedule: ID, owner/workspace, scope, cadence, timezone, next run, enabled state, consent/version, permitted snapshot reference, last/next run, failures, retry policy and delivery preferences.
- ReportComparison: IDs of two immutable report versions, scope compatibility, metric deltas, driver categories and comparability status.
- AuditEvent: event key, entity reference, user/system actor, timestamp, success/failure, safe metadata and correlation/job ID; never raw secrets.
31.2 Entity relationships and invariants
- One farm may contain many fields; a field belongs to one farm at a time, with any relocation represented as a versioned action rather than implicit reassignment.
- A rotation plan belongs to a field; if a farm-level plan spans several fields, each field's sequence and constraints are explicit child plan records.
- One analysis run references exactly one immutable input snapshot.
- One final report references one run and its source/model versions.
- A scheduled run records the exact snapshot/version it used; later local edits do not retroactively alter the report.
- A task may reference a finding but remains a separate record: closing a task does not automatically close an alert or prove improvement.
- Geometry edits create versions; historical analyses reference the geometry version used at calculation time.
- Scenario inputs are isolated from real farm/profile records until a user explicitly applies a reviewed change.
- Source observations are immutable source evidence; normalized/derived values are separate records with transformation/version links.
- Deleted/archived items must not remain selectable as active, but their retention/export behaviour follows the stated deletion policy.
31.3 Explicit status types, not overloaded strings
Use enums/unions for status and availability. For example:
- Data availability: available, partial, stale, unavailable, not_applicable, demo_only.
- Evidence type: observed, satellite_product, modelled, reanalysis, farmer_reported, farmer_confirmed, regional_prior, derived, scenario_assumption, demo.
- Analysis status: draft, queued, validating, fetching_data, quality_control, calculating, generating_explanations, complete, partial, failed, cancelled, retrying.
- Plan status: draft, candidate, active, review_recommended, superseded, archived.
- Alert status: new, acknowledged, snoozed, resolved, dismissed, reopened.
- Task status: suggested, planned, in_progress, completed, deferred, dismissed, cancelled.
Do not encode multiple states into free-form labels and then guess status from text in the UI.
32. Backend architecture, APIs and scheduled jobs
32.1 Local-first with explicit scheduled-analysis bridge
Default farm data lives locally where practical (IndexedDB preferred for structured records and larger local data; localStorage only for compact preferences/selected IDs). The small backend exists for scheduled jobs and any explicitly authorised cloud operations. Because a closed browser cannot be read by a server, the product must define the secure bridge:
1. user enables a schedule;
2. frontend presents the exact minimum dataset required by that schedule;
3. user explicitly consents to transmitting that snapshot;
4. snapshot is encrypted in transit and at rest under access control;
5. backend scheduler operates only on that approved snapshot plus permitted refreshed public source data;
6. each job stores input snapshot version/hash, source and result metadata;
7. results are retrievable only by the workspace owner/authorised client;
8. revoking consent prevents future execution, deletes queued data where possible and follows the disclosed retention policy for past reports;
9. sync conflict rules are explicit if local practices change while an older schedule snapshot is pending.
If the user declines cloud snapshots, local analysis can still be run when the app is open; do not falsely advertise unattended scheduling. Explain the choice with simple language.
32.2 Service separation
Keep clear interfaces among:
- UI/presentation state;
- domain/application service layer;
- typed API adapters;
- analysis orchestrator/job runner;
- individual external data connectors;
- data cleaning/QC and provenance registry;
- crop knowledge base;
- deterministic calculation engine;
- explanation/claim-validation layer;
- job scheduler and notification delivery;
- local persistence and optional cloud persistence;
- reporting/export service.
The scientific engine should be a deterministic, independently testable module with no direct network or UI dependencies. AI services should not directly set scientific scores or invent source observations.
32.3 Minimum API contract families
Implement equivalent endpoints/actions (exact REST names can differ) with typed validation, auth/authorization, request IDs and consistent errors:

Workspace/profile: get/update local or account profile, list/create/update/archive farms, list/create/update/archive fields, geometry validate/save/import/export, list versions.

Wizard: create draft, autosave answer, get/resume draft, validate current answer, prefill optional evidence suggestions, retrieve review model, update inline answer, start analysis.

Analysis: create run, retrieve run, subscribe to progress (SSE/WebSocket/poll fallback), cancel eligible run, retrieve immutable results, retrieve stage/source status, rerun against current inputs.

Rotations: list candidates, get candidate details, compare two/three candidates, set active plan, create plan version, mark review recommended, export plan.

Deep Analysis: run analysis for selected scope, list/retrieve reports, compare reports, retrieve findings, list/create/complete/defer actions, attach note/observation, request report export.

Schedule: create schedule, update cadence/timezone, enable after consent, pause/resume/disable, run now, retrieve job history, revoke snapshot consent.

Simulator: run scenario with validated baseline ID and supported deltas, save/rename/duplicate/delete scenario, compare scenarios, request apply-to-real-plan review.

Sources: list source registry, inspect data coverage/source status for a region/time, retrieve provenance for a metric, inspect methodology version.

All list endpoints need pagination and documented filtering. Validate all ownership relationships on the server. A valid fieldId alone does not prove that it belongs to the requested farmId.
32.4 Job payload and progress events
A job payload includes: run ID, workspace/owner ID, scope IDs, trigger, immutable input snapshot ID/hash, source requests, expected stage names, engine and crop KB versions, timeout/retry policy and idempotency key. Never include raw credentials in a payload visible to the client.

Progress events include runId, stageKey, human-readable stage label, status (pending, running, complete, skipped, partial, failed), start/end times, safe message and completion ratio only where measurable. Stage messages must not leak stack traces or secrets. Percent should reflect actual completed work/weighted stages, not a timer pretending to be progress.

Use exponential backoff with jitter for transient failures, explicit timeouts, limited retries and circuit-breaking/backoff for persistently failing providers. Distinguish rate limit, timeout, unauthorized, invalid response, no coverage and internal calculation error. A retry must not create duplicate reports or notifications.
32.5 Cache strategy and provenance
Cache data by provider/product + native grid cell or geometry lookup + variable/band + start/end time + product/version + processing options. Use source-appropriate TTLs: static historical datasets may be cached long-term; forecasts and recent conditions expire quickly; soil priors persist with product version and retrieval date. Never reuse a cached value from a different geometry/time window without recording how it was aggregated. Cache invalidation must be targeted after geometry, crop, practice or source-version changes.
32.6 Failure, partial success and retry contract
- If one nonessential source fails, continue with valid sources and label the result Partial if the missing source materially affects coverage.
- If a required input/source for a specific calculation is missing, mark that metric unavailable and disable derived actions requiring it.
- If all critical data inputs fail validation, fail the run rather than produce a confident report from defaults.
- Never use a demo value as a silent fallback in a real farm run.
- A rerun creates a new run and report; retrying one failed job retains its identity and attempt history.
- Show what failed, what still succeeded, how it affects the conclusion, and whether retry is useful.
33. Scientific logic, confidence and score governance
33.1 Metric catalogue required before implementing a number
For every displayed score/metric the builder must register:
- stable metric key and human-readable name;
- exact meaning and decision question;
- mathematical formula or reference implementation;
- required inputs, units, temporal range and geometry basis;
- output range/unit and directionality (higher better, lower better, or contextual);
- source evidence type and fallback rules;
- quality filters and missing-data policy;
- model/calculation version;
- validated threshold/interpretation ranges;
- confidence method and known limitations;
- pages/components allowed to display it;
- text used in Simple and Advanced modes;
- tests and evidence/reference citations.
Do not render metrics that do not have this definition. If a metric is planned but not computable, display it in a clearly marked planned/unavailable context rather than inventing a plausible-looking number.
33.2 Crop rotation decision engine
Separate hard constraints from weighted preferences:

Hard constraints reject sequences that violate supported planting windows, crop availability, minimum family return interval, maximum repeated host/crop-family exposure, crop duration constraints, infeasible transition dates, known water constraints or explicitly user-required/excluded crops. Missing optional values should usually reduce confidence rather than act as an invented hard failure, unless there is a clear agronomic/safety constraint.

Candidate generation: produce a diverse set of valid sequences; avoid top N near-duplicates; include meaningful differences in order, family diversity, duration or user-defined constraints. Use deterministic search (e.g. beam search/DP when suitable) with an input-hash seed for tie-breaking. Identical normalized inputs + model versions must produce equivalent ranked outputs.

Candidate score: calculate component metrics separately; normalize each with a documented scale; normalize user weights; clamp weights only when defined by product policy and disclose conflicts; calculate the composite from supported components; retain intermediate values for explanation. Do not make component values appear empirical when they are model-based. A score difference of 1 point should not be described as a meaningful winner unless the method supports such a claim.

Duration and cycle length: evaluate crop durations allowed by the crop knowledge base; penalize unsupported assumptions about perennial crops, declining performance, disease pressure and feasible planting windows. Evaluate total cycle length; disclose what a “season” means for the location/cropping system. Show review timing based on relevant slow-changing soil factors, data confidence and risk triggers. Do not assume every rotation is four seasons or years.

Soil dynamics: only quantify nitrogen, organic matter, carbon or soil-health changes if input/transition models and crop traits are sourced and validated sufficiently. Track units/basis and distinguish a modeled projection from measured soil change. Do not promise microbial activity, disease reduction percentages or nitrogen credits without a transparent evidence basis.

Fallback/output behaviour: if no sequence passes hard constraints, show no acceptable rotation under current assumptions and list realistic inputs/constraints that could be reconsidered. Do not manufacture a recommended crop. Always present downsides and mitigations alongside benefits.
33.3 Water intelligence
Distinguish reference evapotranspiration (ET₀), crop evapotranspiration (ETc), effective rainfall, reported/observed irrigation and estimated root-zone water state. If implementing FAO-56 Penman–Monteith, use the actual required inputs and unit conventions; if using a simpler proxy, label it a proxy and disclose the approximation—never label a temperature-only heuristic as FAO-56. Document crop-coefficient (Kc) and growth-stage assumptions and their source. Show the water-balance period, starting condition, soil water capacity and what is unknown. Do not present rainfall total as identical to effective rainfall.
33.4 Soil and vegetation intelligence
Mapped global soil properties are regional/model estimates with native grid resolution and uncertainty; they are not a laboratory test from the user's exact field. The UI must show property, depth interval, units, source, retrieval/version, prediction uncertainty when available and any gap between field size and map resolution.

Vegetation indicators require actual source imagery/index calculations and product quality controls such as cloud/no-data masks and valid scene dates. NDVI cannot be derived from rainfall. Vegetation trend cannot by itself prove disease, nutrient deficiency or irrigation failure. Where no valid optical data exists, show unavailable/cloud-obscured and retain other evidence.
33.5 Confidence methodology
Confidence is not a decorative percentage. The team must define confidence levels or a calibrated score from measurable factors such as input completeness, source quality, temporal freshness, spatial fit, cross-source consistency and model applicability. Publish the factors and validation strategy. Until calibrated, use evidence labels such as High / Moderate / Limited with an explicit qualitative basis; do not invent a false-precision confidence number.

Missing-data behaviour must be metric-specific. Missing values are not zero. Never interpolate beyond declared limits. If a fallback source replaces a preferred source, preserve both the attempted primary source status and the actual fallback provenance, and lower confidence if appropriate.
33.6 Farm-wide score governance
A farm-wide score is not a required single number until the methodology is defensible. Initially show a farm-wide condition summary plus each field's score/indicators and the worst significant field alerts. If/when a composite index is approved:
- publish components and weights;
- define aggregation by area and whether each component is comparable across fields;
- prevent missing fields silently inflating/deflating the score;
- show coverage and excluded/missing fields;
- retain component and per-field metrics alongside the composite;
- version the score formula and prevent naïve comparisons across incompatible versions.
34. External product research: inspiration to adapt, not copy
Research was performed on publicly available official pages/documentation for farm-management and precision-agriculture products. Borrow interaction patterns and product lessons, not proprietary branding, page screenshots, copy or unique visual compositions.
34.1 OneSoil — field creation and boundary editing
OneSoil documents multiple ways to create fields: draw boundaries, select detected fields where supported, and import field files. It supports vertex edits and saving boundaries; some workflows also use satellite imagery/index layers to refine the boundary. Inspiration for Field Shift: offer a flexible field-creation entry point and a clear review/save cycle, make geometry editing discoverable, and let the farmer improve a boundary instead of treating the first outline as permanent. Adaptation: Field Shift must make source/coverage limitations visible and should not promise automatic detections where no provider exists.

Source: OneSoil — How to add fields; OneSoil — edit field boundaries.
34.2 farmOS — locations, event records and tasks
farmOS connects location assets, logs/events, plans, observations and tasks. Its guide shows that a map can be a navigation entry point into connected records, and that planned tasks can later be marked completed, rescheduled or deleted. Inspiration: Field Shift should use relationships between fields, activities, observations and reports instead of treating each page as isolated data. Adaptation: keep Field Shift's simpler farmer-first UI and specialised evidence/rotation engine; do not force the user to manage a complex raw record database for everyday tasks.

Sources: farmOS User Guide; farmOS Mapping locations; farmOS Logging events.
34.3 John Deere Operations Center — operational continuity
John Deere positions Operations Center around setting up farm operations, planning work, monitoring job quality/productivity and learning from seasonal results; it includes farm maps and field-operation context. Inspiration: connect the recommendation to execution and later review, and make operational status accessible on web/tablet/phone. Adaptation: avoid claims of equipment integrations or near-real-time machine monitoring unless Field Shift actually implements those integrations.

Source: John Deere Operations Center.
34.4 Climate FieldView — comparative field analysis and reports
Climate FieldView describes field-level charts, side-by-side maps, data visualization and custom reports. Inspiration: let Field Shift users compare two rotations, two dated reports or two relevant field layers side by side, and export a clean field/farm brief. Adaptation: comparative visuals must preserve source dates, scale differences and confidence; comparison is not valid when units or datasets are incompatible.

Source: Climate FieldView — Field Health Imagery.
34.5 EOSDA Crop Monitoring — exclusion areas and image selection
EOSDA's user guide documents boundary tools, cutting out parts of a field and choosing available imagery by date. Inspiration: support exclusion zones where needed, explain net cultivable area, and allow selection among actually available valid imagery dates. Adaptation: never assume a current high-resolution image is available for every location/date, and disclose sensor resolution/cloud coverage.

Source: EOSDA Crop Monitoring — Add Field.
34.6 What Field Shift should uniquely contribute
Competitor inspiration is a foundation, not the product's differentiation. Field Shift should be defined by the connected reasoning loop: NASA/soil/climate evidence with explicit quality and resolution; deterministic crop-rotation planning and duration logic; per-field Deep Analysis; farmer practice inputs; transparent “why this changed” reports; scheduled reassessments with consent; and recommendations that become trackable actions without pretending that the software controls the farm.
