Treat the whole document as the master product prompt. Read it all before implementing. Do not cherry-pick only the visually exciting screens. Preserve all the intended capabilities and interactions, explicitly resolve the known contradictions, implement the underlying state/data contracts, and validate the product with the acceptance tests. If the environment cannot implement an integration or background job, do not simulate its success: build the correct interface contract, mark the dependency clearly, and report the block. Do not ask the user to restate requirements that are already in this document. Make professional, reasoned decisions for any gaps, record those assumptions, and continue. The finished application should feel redesigned as one coherent product, not like the old product with a new coat of paint.
39. Global Simple Mode / Advanced Mode contract — mandatory on every page
This is a global presentation mode, not a feature-specific toggle. It is visible in the global header and in Settings. The chosen mode persists between routes, farms, fields and visits within the workspace. After an analysis completes, Simple Mode must show the dismissible invitation described in §39.5.
39.1 Core rule
Both modes consume the same complete FarmAnalysis/canonical report model. The scientific engine and calculations do not change when switching modes. Advanced Mode does not use a different model; it reveals more of the same calculation record. Switching mode must never change a score, reorder rotations, alter a recommendation or invalidate an analysis merely because the display preference changed.

Simple Mode: show the most important conclusion first; plain language; at most 2–3 supporting facts near the headline; no unexplained technical metric; one clear next action; concise supporting context; a “See details” route for every important conclusion.

Advanced Mode: keep every Simple Mode summary, then add the full available numeric results, component scores, raw/source-derived inputs where appropriate, provenance labels, units, date/window, source resolution, quality flags, confidence components, uncertainty range, alternative rankings, historical series, formula, intermediate calculation values, thresholds, assumptions, model/crop knowledge base version and scientific citations. Organise these in tabs, data tables, accordions, evidence drawers and downloadable records so that “show everything” does not mean “dump everything into one screen.” Never hide material limitations in a footnote that is hard to find.
39.2 Mode persistence and transitions
- The single global toggle is always available through Settings and a visible header/profile control.
- When switched, all routes update immediately without losing selected farm, field, current tab, scroll position where sensible, selected candidate, filters, unsaved text or selected report. Never remount the entire app in a way that destroys draft state.
- If the mode changes inside a modal/popup, update the rest of the application and close the popup once the choice is applied.
- When changing from Advanced to Simple, do not delete detailed data; only collapse/hide it.
- A user can drill into an advanced evidence drawer while in Simple Mode. Close the drawer to return to the same Simple Mode page; don't silently flip the global mode.
- Store mode in local preferences and sync across signed-in devices only if account sync is enabled and disclosed.
- Simple/Advanced setting is not a subscription tier. No billing/paywall should be attached to the mode.
39.3 Advanced information organisation
Advanced pages must support progressive disclosure:
1. Human-readable summary (same as Simple Mode).
2. Key metrics and component breakdown.
3. Trends and comparisons.
4. Data table with units, dates, sources and quality status.
5. Calculation/methodology expansion with actual input substitution.
6. Assumptions, confidence and known limitations.
7. Export/print of the relevant evidence record, if implemented.
Use tabs only when they represent different jobs; use accordions for optional detail within the same job. Do not create an accordion for every individual sentence or bury critical risks in an accordion. Use table column configuration/filters for large technical datasets. On mobile, horizontal tables must be inside their own labelled scroll region and include a responsive stacked alternative for the most important data.
39.4 Post-analysis Advanced Mode invitation
After each successfully completed user-triggered analysis that leaves the user in Simple Mode, show a small dismissible popup/card, not a full-screen interruption:
- Title: “Want to explore the full analysis?”
- Body: “Advanced Mode reveals the underlying data, score breakdowns, assumptions and calculation details. Your recommendations will stay the same.”
- Primary action: “Switch to Advanced Mode.”
- Close icon X, plus secondary “Stay in Simple Mode.”
- The card is keyboard accessible, labelled to assistive technology, and does not steal keyboard focus from the analysis result. Escape closes it. The result remains fully usable underneath.
- Clicking Switch updates global mode immediately and reveals Advanced sections on the current result. Clicking X/Stay closes it and leaves the user in Simple Mode.
- The invitation appears after a completed analysis, not after every chart refresh, automatic re-render, schedule heartbeat or retry event. For a scheduled analysis, offer the prompt only when the user opens the completed report in Simple Mode—not as a popup over a background page while no user is present.
- A partial or failed analysis does not present itself as a successful analysis just to show this prompt.
39.5 Mode-specific global header and notifications
Simple header: active farm name/location; notification bell/count; current mode control; Settings; basic notification preferences and units. Simple notification drawer uses plain-language summaries, farm/field name, and the next action.

Advanced header/drawer adds full alert record: source, variable, measurement/value, unit, observation/retrieval time, historical baseline, anomaly magnitude, threshold/version, confidence/evidence type and links to the method. Settings additionally exposes source status, refresh/cache state, engine/crop KB versions and relevant export options.
39.6 Global mode invariants
- Every Simple Mode metric comes from the same source/model as its Advanced equivalent.
- Advanced cannot show a metric that was never computed or is unsupported; it shows an explicit unavailable state plus reason.
- Mode does not change formulas, thresholds, hard constraints or the source selection policy.
- If a score is unavailable, Simple Mode explains the gap in a sentence and Advanced gives the missing inputs and source state.
- If Simple Mode uses “Good / Needs Attention / Alert,” Advanced shows the same status with the supporting component values and thresholds.
40. Exhaustive Home and Farm Tracker content contract
This section turns the original per-mode content requirements into mandatory implementable data and consistency rules.
40.1 Home content — both modes
Farm identity and overall condition: farm name, location, total area where boundary/area is valid, selected farm control, condition label (Good / Needs Attention / Alert / Insufficient data), one-line reason when not Good, last updated timestamp, and a clearly stated data freshness/staleness state.

Current crop: crop name and simple family label (Grain, Legume, Oilseed, etc.), current position written once as “Season 1 of 4” (never duplicate “Season”), stage (sowing, growing, flowering, harvest-ready) only if crop calendar data supports it, days into stage and days until next transition only if the dates are supported, next crop and expected harvest window. Advanced adds recorded planting date and the modelled/reported time actually in the field.

Rotation plan: full sequence with active stage highlighted; each slot explicitly marked as crop, Fallow, or Cover Crop; total cycle length and review point; one short reason for the next crop; link to exact rotation details. Advanced adds match score, evidence confidence and per-crop duration/technical rationale.

Water: plain status (“Enough for current conditions,” “Getting low,” “Critically low,” or “Insufficient data”); one evidence-backed sentence saying what the status means and what to consider doing; recent rainfall and comparison to a suitable baseline; water source (rain-fed, irrigated, both, or unknown); approximate days until water stress only when the water-balance model has suitable inputs; seasonal irrigation allowance used/remaining when the user has entered a limit and usage records exist. Advanced adds topsoil/root-zone moisture values, units/depth, within-field or regional historical percentile when a suitable baseline exists, water-stress days this season, current crop demand in mm versus rainfall/irrigation supply and full calculation basis.

Soil: plain description of known/estimated soil texture; soil condition; moisture compared to usual conditions only where a comparable historical reference exists; potential warnings such as acidic/alkaline, salinity or poor drainage phrased as a possible limitation when based on mapped estimates. Advanced adds pH, organic matter, nitrogen balance in kg N/ha, salinity/EC, drainage classification, soil texture fractions, CEC, available water capacity, bulk density/porosity, slope and their data source, depth, date, native resolution and uncertainty. Do not fabricate all fields if unavailable.

Weather: current/recent temperature and conditions; rain status and recent 24-hour/7-day totals when source cadence supports them; wind and humidity if supplied; comparison to normal only against a correctly defined temporal baseline. A multi-day forecast is labelled Forecast only when a real forecast source provides it. NASA POWER historical/daily data must not be presented as a forecast. Advanced adds highs/lows, solar radiation, hot-day counts, time-window definition and deeper series.

Crop health: Simple may show “vegetation appears above/near/below its recent baseline” only if a real valid satellite-derived observation/index was fetched, quality-screened and appropriate for the field/crop stage. Include cautious wording that vegetation changes can have multiple causes. Advanced adds index name/value, sensor/product, scene date, cloud/quality flags, trend, baseline method, spatial resolution and caveats. If no valid imagery exists, the section shows unavailable/awaiting imagery—not guessed greenness.

Risks and alerts: show only when active risks/alerts exist; prioritise the most urgent; each has what is happening, why it matters and what the farmer can do. Supported classes may include heat, frost, drought/water stress, heavy rain, flooding/waterlogging and pest/disease risk where the model supports the latter. Simple shows Low/Moderate/Elevated plus one reason. Advanced adds value, reference/baseline, anomaly magnitude, source, timestamp, threshold and evidence confidence. If there are no alerts, remove the section entirely or show a compact neutral state only if needed to explain monitoring is active; never fill space with fake alerts.

What to do next: one highest-priority evidence-backed action, or “No immediate action identified from the available data” only when all evaluated status indicators are non-concerning and enough data exists. Also show upcoming tasks in the next 1–2 weeks, such as irrigation review, sowing window, harvest window, field inspection or soil test due, each with a farm-specific reason. Advanced adds exact windows/dates, supporting calculation and dependencies.

Trends: 30-day soil-moisture trend only if comparable observations/model outputs exist; monthly rainfall vs normal with baseline; meaningful persistent changes with duration/magnitude. Advanced adds season-to-date charts, same-season historical comparisons and year-by-year values, and marks unavailable time windows.

Trust: confidence/evidence-quality label and one-line reason; key data source type (satellite, farmer-entered, estimated, calculated); data freshness for weather, moisture, and crop health; relevant missing field profile inputs and how to add them. Advanced adds confidence contributors, provenance tag on every value, native resolution, fallbacks, model version and details in the Evidence Drawer.

Linked actions: open full rotation detail; open exact field detail; stress-test selected baseline in Simulator; edit farm/field details; open Deep Analysis; add observation; review tasks.
40.2 Overall-status consistency rule
All related status phrases must be derived from a single status evaluation object/version. The headline condition, water/soil labels, descriptions, suggested action and alert severity cannot be independently hardcoded. The overall farm/field condition may not be more positive than a qualifying critical underlying indicator. If a critical indicator is stale or unverified, show an appropriate caution/unknown state instead of a false “Good.”

Required regression cases from the attachment:
- Water status cannot say “Adequate” while its detail says “critically low, irrigation needed.”
- Soil status cannot say “Balanced” while the explanatory sentence contradicts it without explicitly distinguishing different measures.
- “Nothing needed right now” cannot coexist with a credible critical water state.
- The header cannot say “Good Condition” when a verified critical alert is active.
- Rotation position must not repeat terms (“Year 1 • Season 1 of 4,” not “Season 1 Season”).
- If data is missing, do not choose Good simply because no alert calculation ran.
Implement StatusAssessment once and feed every surface from it; test contradictions at the domain/unit level and in the UI.
40.3 My Farms portfolio modes
Simple mode lists farm name, location, area, current crop/rotation state and Good/Needs Attention/Alert label. Advanced adds total managed area, active farm count, field/rotation review count, alerts and portfolio metrics only when scientifically comparable. An average soil-health figure is allowed only if the underlying metrics have the same definition, depth, time basis and source/model version; otherwise show per-field distributions and coverage instead.
40.4 Farm detail expanded history and action plan
Simple shows Home information expanded into readable sentences, everyday soil/water descriptions, rotation sequence and one-line reason for each placement, plus a short “Why this was chosen.” Advanced adds:
- Soil & Satellite Profile: soil texture fractions, pH, organic matter, CEC, available water capacity, bulk density/porosity, slope/class, salinity/EC, drainage class and underlying 10-year rainfall series when valid; each value carries provenance, confidence/uncertainty, depth, date, units and resolution.
- Rotations & Schedule: all season-by-season entries with Year and Season separate, planting/harvest windows, days in field, six component scores per rotation (suitability, water, soil health, resilience, productivity, diversity) when available.
- Historical Crop Records: prior planted crops by season, actual dates where supplied, recorded pest/disease issues, yields only when farmer-provided or connected to a valid source, and provenance.
- Agronomic Action Plan: checklist of soil tests, cover-crop termination windows, sowing windows, transitions and other operations derived from the actual selected plan's timeline and local constraints.
- Full “Why this was chosen”: formulas/score drivers, assumptions, source list, rejected alternatives, limitations, uncertainty and why the crop order/durations were selected.
40.5 Farmer journal and performance history
Allow a simple note/observation field for issues such as a pest sighting, irrigation-system problem, visible dry patch, planting/harvest completion or management event. Capture date, field, optional quantity/unit, text and optional image attachment. Ask whether the note describes something that happened or a plan/intention. Do not parse a report of a broken pump into “irrigation definitely stopped for 3 days” unless the user states that duration. Advanced can structure quantities and show parsed fields for confirmation.

Yield/performance records are optional farmer-provided events with crop/season, quantity/unit, area basis, measurement method and date; never invent yields from a suitability score. Compare planned vs reported performance only when definitions/units/area/time windows align.
41. Exhaustive Crop Rotation output data contract
41.1 Simple Mode output
- Recommended rotation sequence, with current/active stage distinct.
- A plain-language overall label (“Strong fit for your current priorities”) with numerical score optional but never alone.
- Confidence label and one short reason.
- Total cycle length described in growing seasons/years correctly, with review timing.
- A clear choice of Fallow or Cover Crop, never the ambiguous combined option. Explain what each does in ordinary language.
- Three to four benefits grounded in the model and actual crop sequence.
- Simple expected impact directions for yield/productivity potential, soil direction, water use/demand and resilience. Numerical deltas are optional details and should only appear if supported.
- Crop family tag in plain words and a short explanation why family diversity matters.
- One to two alternative plans with their sequence and why they may be useful.
- Rejected combinations grouped behind an expandable section; simple view shows the count only if candidate generation actually evaluated that many candidates.
- Next action: compare, explore an explicitly hypothetical scenario, see field detail or save the plan.
- Dedicated Benefits & Risks section with disadvantage, risk level, affected stage, management mitigation and caveat; never only positive claims.
41.2 Advanced Mode output — complete detail
For every candidate, display the six component scores (suitability, water, soil health, resilience, productivity, diversity) and their definitions/units or scales. Display confidence contributors: input completeness, data quality, rank stability, crop-parameter coverage, field/grid scale fit and climate representativeness—but only if each has an implemented, defined measure. Show score uncertainty range/percentile interval and proportion of modelled test scenarios in which the candidate ranks first only if those simulations actually ran and the sample/method are stated.

Season/crop detail includes: distinct Year and Season fields; planting window and harvest window; crop duration/days in field; climate suitability for relevant crop phase; water demand in mm vs available precipitation/irrigation envelope; expected water-stress days if modelled; nitrogen addition/removal in kg N/ha and decay of legume credit across later seasons only with sourced coefficients; organic matter trajectory per season only if the soil model supports it; pest/disease break explanation for specific supported host/pathogen relationships; pH, drainage, salinity, slope and growing-period suitability factors with limiting factor, each only when inputs/knowledge base permit.

Rejected rotation detail for each actual rejected candidate includes failed constraint, actual computed value, threshold, difference to threshold and whether the failure is due to user constraint, climate, crop data, sequence feasibility or insufficient information. “Rejected” and “not assessed” are distinct states.

Explain total cycle-length choice versus alternatives and score difference; explain why each perennial/multi-season crop stays for its selected duration; explain review date and triggers; explain order dependence (what the previous crop contributes/consumes and what the following crop gains); show source/provenance on every number; show technical explanation alongside plain-language summary.
41.3 Fallow versus cover crop logic
Never label a stage as “Cover Crop/Fallow” without deciding which one the plan actually contains. A cover crop is a planted crop with a crop family, duration and purpose; fallow is a non-cropped/rest period with its own assumptions and potential weed/moisture considerations. The engine must choose based on supported goals/constraints and present the reason. Do not generalise “always choose fallow in arid climates” or “always choose cover crop when moisture is high” as an unqualified rule; use crop/soil/climate-specific criteria and validate it. If evidence is insufficient to select confidently, show the alternatives and ask the user or mark the choice tentative.
41.4 Water, soil and disease claims
- Crop water demand and supply use compatible periods and units; irrigation and rainfall are distinct terms.
- Nitrogen credit is not a universal fixed figure for every legume/soil/biomass; source assumptions and uncertainty are visible.
- Soil organic matter cannot be claimed to rise by a fixed percentage from rotation alone without a model, initial condition and documented transitions.
- Disease interruption depends on crop family, pathogen/host range, region and management. If the knowledge base cannot establish a relevant pathogen, use a general diversity explanation, not an invented species-specific percentage.
- Yield impact is potential/model-based unless supported by verified local data; never state a harvest guarantee.
41.5 Financial detail, separately gated
Gross margin, seed/fertilizer/fuel cost, commodity prices and market shocks are Advanced Mode only, separate from the agronomic match score, and later-phase unless a disclosed credible local/regional source and calculation method are implemented. Every price contains currency, unit, location/market, observation date and source. Until then, financial examples must be explicitly Demo / illustrative; they must never look like actual local quotes or predictions.
42. Exhaustive Simulator, Calendar, Data and Setup mode-specific requirements
42.1 What-If Climate Simulator
Simple: a small set of plain-language presets/controls for hotter conditions, less rain or lower available irrigation where supported; one before/after explanation; whether the top rotation changes; a clear “hypothetical scenario, not a forecast” disclosure.

Advanced: all candidate score deltas; rank movement; which rotations newly fail a hard constraint; driver attribution by temperature/rainfall/water supply; baseline vs scenario water-balance chart and raw values; saved scenario parameter set and source/model snapshot; uncertainty or sensitivity only if computed. Saving/renaming/deleting/comparing a scenario must work. All hypothetical values carry a scenario provenance tag and stay isolated from real data until explicit reviewed application.
42.2 Economic / market shock simulation
This is a separate future module, not blended into the climate simulator or agronomic score. Simple presets might express a qualitative effect if a supported model exists; Advanced provides crop-level fertilizer/commodity price deltas, gross margin and uncertainty. It remains unavailable or clearly illustrative until a valid non-NASA market/input cost provider, local currency and model are implemented. Do not make up a claim that a rotation “will still profit.”
42.3 Calendar
Simple: month view with crop names shown on planted/planned days; selected date displays crop stage and one or two evidence-grounded recommended activities; a crop/field colour legend and labels; view/event details are clickable.

Advanced: full planting/harvest windows per crop, year and season, task timing rationale, overlapping schedules across fields/farms, historical actual dates vs current plan if records exist, linked crop water/soil/climate reasoning. Events from different farms must remain distinguishable even when the same crop occurs on the same date. Each event carries provenance (planned, farmer-recorded actual, modelled window) and must not look like a confirmed real event if it is only planned.
42.4 Data & Trust Centre
Simple: clear list of sources actually used; short plain-language description of how recommendations are made; privacy/local storage explanation; “what is missing?” summary.

Advanced: full dataset/product/mission registry; units/variables; native spatial/temporal resolution; period coverage; retrieval status and most recent successful time; source documentation; calculation formulas and all required inputs/assumptions; crop-parameter sources; exact provenance record from current analysis; exportable audit log. “Working / Degraded / Unavailable / Planned” is dynamic and based on actual integration health.
42.5 Setup and re-analysis wizard
Simple: only required location/field, soil, water, crop history, planning horizon and priorities; optional accuracy stage described as “Answer a few extra questions to improve the assessment.” Use everyday wording and plain priority sliders.

Advanced: full soil lab measurements with named nutrients and units, pH scale, salinity/EC, exact irrigation/tillage terminology, structured crop management inputs, raw normalized answer data and the precise confidence factor influenced by each answer. Review must show both farmer-entered raw value and normalized representation where appropriate. Editing any answer triggers dependency analysis and flags affected results stale, but must not silently recompute and overwrite previous reports.
42.6 Crop suitability panel
Where shown, list candidate crops and rank only those evaluated. Include a factor breakdown for supported temperature/season, water, soil pH, drainage, salinity, slope and growing-period fit; identify the limiting factor; provide provenance/confidence; let user filter by family, suitability or constraints; and show why excluded/unknown candidates cannot be scored. Ensure this is not a detached duplicate engine: its scores come from the same versioned model as the rotation planner.
43. Data freshness, change propagation and calculation dependency registry
Implement a dependency map so every change invalidates the right results and no more than necessary. Example dependency chain:
- Boundary geometry → area/centroid and spatial aggregation → soil/grid overlap and vegetation aggregation → confidence/coverage → field status, water/soil context, rotation ranking if field size/location meaningfully changes, Deep Analysis baseline. It does not change historical reports.
- Soil lab values/profile → soil suitability, pH/salinity/drainage constraints, applicable water-holding model, rotation score and report.
- Crop history → family-return intervals, disease-cycle logic, rotation generation and current sequence context.
- Irrigation source/limit/routine → water balance, crop suitability, water risk, action priorities, Deep Analysis, schedule input snapshot.
- Recorded irrigation event → actual management history and water balance for its stated period; do not infer unrecorded events.
- Rainfall observation/reanalysis → relevant water balance and weather trend; must not imply irrigation status.
- Forecast update → forward-looking planning windows/risks; does not rewrite observed historical rainfall.
- Crop plan/date change → crop stage, calendar, per-stage climate/water/risk model and active-plan status.
- Crop knowledge base/model version → candidate scores and comparability flags; generate a new run, retain the earlier version.
- Display mode/units → presentation only, not scientific outputs.
- Scenario slider → scenario branch only, not real farm records.
For each value, store observedAt, retrievedAt, validFrom/To if relevant, sourceProductVersion, qualityStatus, staleAfter/TTL, and the dependency/engine version. A UI refresh does not magically make old source data newer. Show “checked now; latest available observation is dated …” when applicable.

When an input changes, display a small banner on affected result screens: “This assessment uses the previous irrigation setup. Update the analysis to use your new information.” Buttons: “Review changes,” “Run updated analysis,” “Keep viewing previous report.” If viewing an immutable historic report, do not overlay current values and make the old report appear updated.
